  "root_cause_analysis": "The model conflated file-moving logic with state verification, repeatedly attempting mv/cp with different path combinations rather than committing to one atomic operation. This consumed inference budget without advancing the conversation state. Additionally, returning narrative text in Turn 0 violated the requirement to return only tool execution output tuples, signaling a misunderstanding of multi-turn state mutation semantics.",
  "correct_approach": "Turn 0: Execute mkdir('Projects') and pwd atomically; return only tool output tuples, no commentary. Turn 1: Execute one atomic mv or cp call to move/rename the proposal file; immediately return results and await next user input. Turns 2–5: Execute touch('notes.md'), echo('Hello') with redirection to summary.txt, diff, and wc in sequence without looping or narrative padding.",
  "key_insight": "Multi-turn file-system tasks require strict adherence to atomic operations and pure tool-output responses (delta6). Avoid retry loops and narrative substitution; each turn should execute exactly the operations implied by the user request, return tool results, and yield control for the next turn."
}

